.Dd October 30, 2026
.Dt ARNO-SERVE 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm arno-serve
.Nd the messages a program speaks to an arno session
.Sh DESCRIPTION
An
.Xr arno 1
session can be driven or watched by another program.
There are two ways in, and one language for both:
.Bl -tag -width "a viewer"
.It "a host"
starts
.Ql "arno --serve"
as a child and talks to it over the child's standard input and output.
It owns the session: it types the lines, answers the questions, and may give
the model tools of its own.
A word processor and a window around arno are hosts.
.It "a viewer"
connects to the session's socket, and sees what the session shows.
It may type lines and stop the turn, but it is never asked a question.
.Ql "arno --attach"
is one.
.El
.Pp
This page is the protocol: the framing, then the host's side, then the
viewer's.
The text of what the model and the tools say is in
.Xr arno 1 ;
here are only the messages that carry it.
.Sh FRAMING
Every message, in both directions, is one netstring whose contents are a run
of netstrings, the verb first.
A netstring is a length in decimal, a colon, that many bytes and a comma:
.Ql 5:hello, .
So the message
.Cm line
with the text
.Ql hi
is
.Bd -literal -offset indent
12:4:line,2:hi,,
.Ed
.Pp
and a message with an empty field, such as the answer
.Cm ok
with three, is written with
.Ql 0:, .
Numbers inside messages are decimal text.
.Pp
A length of more than nine digits, or of more than 64 MiB, is malformed, and
so is a message whose netstrings do not fill it exactly.
Whoever reads one cannot read on: a host is told
.Cm error ,
and the session ends; a viewer is dropped.
.Pp
Bytes are not interpreted: a field holds whatever text it holds, newlines and
all.
.Sh THE HOST
.Ss Starting
The host runs
.Ql "arno --serve" ,
which takes no other argument, with pipes for standard input and output.
Standard error is left for crashes.
Nothing but messages is written to the host's pipe:
.Nm arno
moves its own standard output to a copy of standard error, so that nothing a
tool prints can land in the middle of a message.
.Pp
The session ends when the host says
.Cm bye
or closes the pipe, and
.Nm arno
exits with status 0.
A host that goes away while a question waits refuses it.
.Pp
Messages that come before
.Cm hello
are answered with
.Cm error
.Ql "hello first" .
Messages that come after
.Cm hello
wait until the session is made.
.Ss Messages the host sends
.Bl -tag -width "setsystem id text"
.It Cm hello Ar version name root json
First, and once.
.Ar version
is
.Ql 1 ;
any other is answered
.Cm error
.Ql "protocol version V unknown; this arno speaks 1"
and the session ends.
.Ar name
is the host's name, which the model is told and which a viewer's lister
shows as the owner; empty is
.Ql "the host" .
.Ar root
is the project root: arno works there, and lets its file tools reach that
project without asking, as from a terminal in that directory.
Empty leaves it where it started; one that cannot be entered is answered
.Cm error
.Ql "cannot work in ROOT"
and ends the session.
.Pp
.Ar json
is an object, every member optional; an empty field is the empty object, and
anything else ends the session with
.Cm error
.Ql "hello's last field must be a JSON object" :
.Bl -tag -width "builtins"
.It Cm prompt
A section for the model's system prompt: what the host is, what its tools are
for.
.It Cm tools
The host's own tools, an array of objects with
.Cm name ,
.Cm description
and
.Cm parameters ,
the last a JSON Schema object, passed on to the model as it stands.
A tool with no name, or with the name of one of arno's own tools, of an MCP
server's, or of an earlier host tool, is refused with
.Cm error
.Ql "tool NAME is already arno's own"
or the like, and the rest go ahead.
.It Cm builtins
An array of the names of arno's own tools that the host allows.
A builtin left out is gone: the model neither sees it nor can call it.
Missing allows them all; an empty array allows none.
The tools of MCP servers are not affected.
.El
.It Cm line Ar text
A line of input, as typed: a message to the model, or a command that starts
with a slash.
Lines that come while a turn runs wait for it to end, in the session's
queue, the host's and viewers' in the order they came; see
.Cm queue .
An empty line is ignored.
.Ic /quit
and
.Ic /exit
end the session.
.It Cm cancel
Stop the turn, as C-c does.
A question or a tool call that waits is refused, which ends the turn too.
With no turn running it does nothing.
.It Cm answer Ar id key Op Ar reason
The answer to
.Cm ask
number
.Ar id .
.Ar key
is one byte, one of the keys the question offered; anything else is a refusal.
.Ar reason
may go with a refusal, and the model reads it as it would the person's:
.Ql "declined by user: too long" .
An answer to an id that is not waited on is dropped.
.It Cm result Ar id text
The result of
.Cm call
number
.Ar id ,
for the model to read.
Dropped like an answer for an id not waited on.
.It "recent, paste, rename, forget, models, system, setsystem"
Requests about the conversations, with the fields the section
.Sx Requests
gives each.
.It Cm bye
End the session.
.El
.Pp
A message that has fewer fields than its verb needs is answered
.Cm error
.Ql "VERB needs N fields" ;
extra fields are ignored.
A verb arno does not know is answered
.Cm error
.Ql "unknown verb VERB" ,
so that a host newer than this arno finds out what it may not use.
A second
.Cm hello
is answered
.Cm error
.Ql "hello again" .
.Ss Messages arno sends
.Bl -tag -width "systemset id"
.It Cm ready Ar conversation model pid
After
.Cm hello ,
once the session is made.
.Ar conversation
is the id of the current conversation,
.Ar model
the model in use,
.Ar pid
arno's process id.
.It Cm push Ar n kind text Op Ar worker model
A new entry of the transcript.
.Ar n
is its index, from 0, the same counter for every kind of entry.
.Ar kind
is
.Ql user
for what was typed,
.Ql asst
for the model's reply,
.Ql info
for what arno says on the side, or
.Ql err
for an error.
The text of a reply is often empty at first, and grows by
.Cm append .
.Ar worker ,
when present, is the number of the worker that said it, in decimal text.
It is left out for the lead's own entries, so a host that
ignores it, or reads fields by position, is not affected.
A worker's number is the one its changes are kept and merged by, when it
works in a copy of its own, and counts on from there when it does not.
.Ar model ,
after it, is the model the worker runs on, which a screen labels its
entries with.
Both apply to
.Cm tool
too.
.It Cm tool Ar n name args Op Ar worker model
A new entry, number
.Ar n ,
for a tool call: its name and its arguments as JSON.
It comes before the call runs, so before any
.Cm ask
about it: an edit's path and text are in its arguments by the time its
question comes.
.It Cm append Ar n text
More text for entry
.Ar n ,
the reply streaming.
It is not always the last entry: workers side by side each stream into a
reply of their own, between each other's entries, so a host adds the
text to entry
.Ar n ,
never simply to the last.
.It Cm done Ar n text
The result of tool call
.Ar n ,
which, with workers side by side, need not be the last.
.It Cm reset
The transcript is empty again, after
.Ic /new ,
.Ic /load
or
.Ic /clear ;
what follows rebuilds it.
A host that keeps the transcript appends what it is told, in order, and has
the same numbers.
.It Cm status Ar text
The status line: mode, with the safety and, in build, the plan; then
model, context in use, tokens, cost, and the title.
.It Cm busy Ar 0|1
1 when a turn of the model starts and 0 when it ends, once the conversation
is saved.
A command, or the work arno does before the first turn, is not a turn and is
not told.
.It Cm ask Ar id question detail keys
A question for the person, which the host must answer with
.Cm answer .
.Ar id
is its number, counted from 1.
.Ar question
is the question and
.Ar detail
what to show with it, an edit's diff or the lines a command would run, or
empty.
.Ar keys
are the bytes that may answer it, such as
.Ql yn
or
.Ql yna ;
the first that is not
.Ql y
is the refusal.
Every question is put to the host: an edit, a path outside the project, a
program to run, a call of an MCP server's tool, whether to send the context
files, and the stage's diff.
.It Cm call Ar id name args
A call of one of the host's tools, which the host must answer with
.Cm result .
.Ar args
is JSON, as the model wrote it.
The ids of
.Cm ask
and
.Cm call
share a counter, and one is waited on at a time.
If a cancel comes, or the host goes, the call's result is
.Ql "interrupted by user" ,
or
.Ql "error: NAME has gone away" .
.It Cm queue Ar held Op Ar line ...
The lines that wait for the turn running, in the order they will run,
each its own field; none when the queue is empty.
It comes when the queue changes, once arno has started what it can, so a
line that runs at once is never told as waiting.
.Ar held
is 1 when a terminal's
.Nm arno
holds the queue after a turn that ended on an error or a stop, until an
Enter; else 0.
With
.Fl \-serve
it is always 0: the host says what runs.
.It Cm spent Ar n cost
What worker
.Ar n
has spent so far, at list price, as
.Ic /usage
writes dollars:
.Ql $0.042 .
It comes after each request of the worker's, for the line that says
the worker in the lead's view.
.It Cm error Ar text
What arno made of the host's last message: see above; or, to a request, why it
cannot be done.
.El
.Pp
A newer arno may send verbs this page does not list; a host ignores them.
.Pp
The answers to requests are in the next section.
.Ss Requests
These are answered at once, even while a turn runs, in the order asked:
.Cm recent ,
.Cm paste ,
.Cm forget ,
and
.Cm rename ,
.Cm system
and
.Cm setsystem
of a saved conversation.
.Cm models
and the same three for the current conversation, which a running job may be
changing, wait for the job's end and are answered then.
So that replies come in the order of the requests, every request that comes
while another waits waits too.
.Pp
An empty
.Ar id
names the current conversation, whose id a host cannot know after
.Ic /new
or
.Ic /load .
The answer to
.Cm rename ,
.Cm system
or
.Cm setsystem
then carries its id.
.Bl -tag -width "setsystem id text"
.It Cm recent Ar max filter
At most
.Ar max
saved conversations, the one changed last first, those whose title or id holds
.Ar filter ,
ignoring case; an empty
.Ar filter
is all.
Answered
.Cm recent Ar json :
an array of objects with
.Cm id ,
.Cm title
and
.Cm updated ,
the time of the last change in seconds since 1970.
A
.Ar max
that is not a number of 1 or more is answered
.Cm error
.Ql "recent needs a count of 1 or more" .
.It Cm paste
Save the clipboard's PNG, as C-v does in the terminal.
Answered
.Cm pasted Ar path ,
the file, for a line to name with
.Ql @path ;
or
.Cm error
with why there is none.
The file is removed as the session ends.
.It Cm rename Ar id title
Name a saved conversation, or the current one; an empty
.Ar title
clears the name.
Answered
.Cm renamed Ar id .
The current one's new title shows on the status line.
.It Cm forget Ar id
Remove a saved conversation and its scratch directory.
Answered
.Cm forgot Ar id .
The current conversation is refused:
.Cm error
.Ql "the current conversation cannot be forgotten" .
.It Cm models
The models
.Ic /models
would list.
Answered
.Cm models Ar json :
an object with
.Cm provider ,
.Cm model
and
.Cm models ,
an array, empty for a provider with no list.
.It Cm system Ar id
A conversation's own system prompt.
Answered
.Cm system Ar json :
an object with
.Cm id ,
.Cm prompt ,
the conversation's own, empty when it has none, and
.Cm default ,
arno's own instructions, which it replaces.
.It Cm setsystem Ar id text
Set a conversation's own system prompt, of any length and lines; empty goes
back to arno's own.
Answered
.Cm systemset Ar id .
The current conversation's takes effect from its next request.
.El
.Pp
A
.Cm rename ,
.Cm forget ,
.Cm system
or
.Cm setsystem
of a conversation that is not saved is answered
.Cm error
.Ql "no conversation ID" .
.Sh THE VIEWER
Every arno on a terminal, and every one run with
.Fl \-serve ,
listens on a Unix socket for viewers:
.Pa $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/arno/PID.view ,
or, with no such directory,
.Pa run/
in arno's cache directory,
.Pa ~/.cache/arno/ .
The directory is made with mode 700, so that only its user can enter it, and a
connection from another user is dropped.
.Pp
A connection says what it is by its first netstring, which is not a message:
.Bl -tag -width "3:who,"
.It Li 6:attach,
A viewer.
It keeps the connection.
arno sends it, as to a host: a
.Cm reset ;
the transcript so far, as
.Cm push
and
.Cm tool
and
.Cm done ,
a reply that is still streaming as far as it has got; the
.Cm status ;
and
.Cm busy ;
and
.Cm queue ,
if lines wait;
and
.Cm listed ,
if there are models; and
.Cm spent
for each worker that has spent anything.
From then on it gets every change, in the host's own messages
.Pq Cm push , tool , append , done , reset , status , busy , queue , spent
and in
.Cm listed .
.Pp
.Cm listed Ar models
is the models
.Ic /model
completes from, a line each, as the session has them when no job runs;
it comes again when they change, as a job ends.
It is a viewer's alone: a host asks with
.Cm models .
.Pp
It may send
.Cm line ,
as the host does, which joins the queue while a turn runs;
.Cm cancel ;
and
.Cm unqueue Ar text .
An empty
.Cm line
lets go of a held queue.
A viewer's
.Cm cancel
stops the turn, as the host's does; with none running, or one stopping
already, it empties the queue, as a second C-c does.
.Cm unqueue
takes the last line out of the queue, if it is still
.Ar text ,
for the viewer to edit.
A
.Cm line
of
.Ql /usage
while a turn runs is answered at once, not queued: the counters as they
stood when the turn began, in an
.Ql info
entry, which every screen and the host are told, since the transcript and
its numbers are one for all.
Anything else it sends is ignored.
A viewer is never sent
.Cm ask ,
.Cm call ,
.Cm ready
or
.Cm error ;
while a question waits, its
.Cm status
says so, with
.Ql "a question waits in WHO"
added, and the answer shows in the tool call's
.Cm done .
.It Li 3:who,
A lister.
It is answered with one message,
.Cm ok Ar owner root title ,
and the connection is closed.
.Ar owner
is the host's name, or
.Ql terminal
for an arno on a terminal;
.Ar root
the project root;
.Ar title
the conversation's title.
.El
.Pp
A viewer that falls more than a megabyte behind is dropped, so that a stuck
terminal never holds up the session.
Any other first netstring, or a malformed message, drops the connection.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A host says hello with one tool, and is told the session is ready:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
host -> arno   hello 1 pad "" {"prompt":"pad is a scratch buffer.",
                              "builtins":["read","grep"],
                              "tools":[{"name":"doc_read",
                                        "description":"The buffer."}]}
arno -> host   ready 20261028-101500 deepseek-v4.1-flash:cloud 4242
arno -> host   status "arno · chat @ ollama · ctx ..."
.Ed
.Pp
A line, a streaming reply, and a question:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
host -> arno   line "fix the typo"
arno -> host   busy 1
arno -> host   push 0 user "fix the typo"
arno -> host   push 1 asst ""
arno -> host   append 1 "Looking at "
arno -> host   tool 2 doc_read {}
arno -> host   call 1 doc_read {}
host -> arno   result 1 "1  teh quick fox"
arno -> host   done 2 "1  teh quick fox"
arno -> host   ask 2 "apply these changes?" "diff..." yn
host -> arno   answer 2 n "keep teh"
.Ed
.Pp
List the live sessions: a line for each, with its process id, owner, project
root and conversation title, separated by tabs:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ arno \-\-attach \-l
4242	terminal	/home/me/proj	fix the typo
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr arno 1 ,
.Xr arno-peer 5
.Pp
D. J. Bernstein,
.Lk https://cr.yp.to/proto/netstrings.txt "Netstrings" ,
1997.
.Sh HISTORY
The framing and the host's messages are those the Pascal arno's
.Fl \-serve
spoke, so that a host written for it works with this arno.
.Pp
The protocol of the editors that talk to arno by a socket of their own,
as a peer, is a different one, in
.Xr arno-peer 5 .
.Sh CAVEATS
A viewer sees that a question waits and who holds it, not the question.
.Pp
A viewer that is slow is sent changes when the socket takes them; a tail that
is not yet sent waits for the next change.
.Pp
A turn stops at the next read of the model's reply, so a
.Cm cancel
may take as long as the model's next piece of text.
